<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Central Lithuania</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1922 CE · capital Vilnius</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Polish-controlled state around Vilnius, created by a staged mutiny in 1920 and annexed by Poland in 1922.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In October 1920, days after an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, the Polish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski">Lucjan Zeligowski</a> led troops in a staged mutiny, approved secretly by Pilsudski, and captured the city. He proclaimed the Republic of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, claiming to act for the local Polish-speaking population.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zeligowski ruled as supreme commander through a provisional governing commission, and the state had its own stamps, army and courts. Its population was mixed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> attempts to arrange a plebiscite failed, and elections in January 1922 were boycotted by most Lithuanians, Jews and Belarusians.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The resulting assembly voted in February 1922 to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the territory became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> refused to recognise this and broke off relations until 1938, regaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1939.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-central-lithuania.jpg" alt="Republic of Central Lithuania" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Лобачев Владимир · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Central_Lithuania_1920.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1922 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vilnius</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lucjan Zeligowski</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Lithuania, Belarus</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Central Lithuania, Litwa Srodkowa</dd></dl></aside></div>
